Ref: https://github.com/eclipse/jnosql/issues/400

## Changes

- Added no-args constructor into the injectable beans;

Closes https://github.com/AngelProtocolFinance/evm-smart-contracts/issues/150

## Explanation of the solution
- adds additional `expect`s to endowment creation
- adds missing test assertions for `AccountsUpdate` https://github.com/AngelProtocolFinance/evm-smart-contracts/pull/167#discussion_r1252363895 and https://github.com/AngelProtocolFinance/evm-smart-contracts/pull/167#discussion_r1252364193
- adds `chai` plugins for validating certain endowment fields' equality:
  - the problem this is trying to solve is that Solidity contracts return tuples as their results, so the resulting type is some weird combination of array and object. A good example of this is `FeeSettingStructOutput`, which is defined as:
  ```
  export type FeeSettingStructOutput = [string, BigNumber] & {
    payoutAddress: string;
    bps: BigNumber;
  };
  ```
  On the other hand, all types that are used to **call** contracts have the normal `object`-like type, e.g. `FeeSettingStruct`:
  ```
  export type FeeSettingStruct = {
    payoutAddress: PromiseOrValue<string>;
    bps: PromiseOrValue<BigNumberish>;
  };
  ```
  Because of this, we can't use in-built `chai` methods to assert deep equality between these two, so we solve the issue by creating a [chai plugin](https://www.chaijs.com/guide/plugins/), resulting in much shorter test assertions.
  Created plugins for `FeeSettingStructOutput` and for `SettingsPermissionStructOutput`.
## Instructions on making this work

- run `yarn` or `yarn install` to install npm dependencies
- run `yarn test` to verify all tests still pass

